// The controls package: cameras steered by input. A controller reads the
// input package's actions, never a device, and gives back a Pose: where the
// camera is and which way it faces. The program applies the pose to what it
// draws with (render's Camera: 'camera.origin = pose.origin;
// camera.orientation = pose.orientation'), so the controller owns no camera,
// and a camera that is one day a part attached to another (through
// someone's eyes, following someone) changes only who applies the pose, and
// in whose coordinates: a pose is relative to whatever the program places it
// in (render's Camera already follows a placement, from an eye).
//
// - Orbit: round a target. A drag with the left button turns it, the wheel
//   zooms (each notch away from the user 10 % nearer), a drag with the
//   right or middle button pans the target across the view.
// - Fly: free. W, A, S, D move forward, left, back and right along the
//   view (by scancode, so they are the same keys on AZERTY), Space rises,
//   Ctrl sinks, and the mouse looks (in relative mode, which the program
//   switches on: 'wantsRelativeMouse').
// - CameraControls: both, and C toggles between them, the new one taking
//   the view the old one left.
//
//   mut ctx = Context();
//   imm actions = CameraActions.bind(&mut ctx);       // the standard bindings
//   mut controls = CameraControls.make(actions, Orbit.fromEye(eye, target), &mut ctx);
//   ... each frame, the events drained into 'input' and 'ctx.update(&input)':
//   controls.update(&mut ctx, dt);                      // just before the view is built
//   imm pose = controls.pose();
//   camera.origin = pose.origin;
//   camera.orientation = pose.orientation;
//
// Look is applied as late as possible: 'update' is meant to be called just
// before the view matrix is built, after everything else of the frame that
// does not depend on the camera, so the newest input moves it.
//
// Conventions are geomath's: right-handed, y up, a camera looking down its
// own -z; angles in radians. A yaw of 0 looks down -z (an orbit's eye is on
// the target's +z side); a yaw turns about +y, counterclockwise seen from
// above. An orbit's pitch is its eye's height above the target's horizon; a
// fly camera's pitch is how far it looks up. Each is kept within
// MAX_PITCH of level, short of straight up or down, where 'lookAt' has no
// sideways.

